WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.080
RYAN MATSUMOTO: Hi.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.710
My name is Ryan, and this is an
introduction to Google Cloud.

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.230
This video is great for anyone
new to Google Cloud who's

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.210
interested in learning more.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:12.150
In the next few minutes, we'll
talk about what Google Cloud is

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.400
and how it can help
you as a developer.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:15.210
Ready?

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:16.517
Let's get started.

00:00:16.517 --> 00:00:19.786
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:23.730
Cloud computing is all
about getting things done

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.170
using someone else's computers.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:27.810
So when you use
Google Cloud, you're

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.840
getting things done
using Google's computers.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:34.230
Google Cloud lets you build in
host applications, store data,

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.600
and analyze data, all
in Google's highly

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:40.600
scalable and reliable
computing infrastructure.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:43.970
Google's data centers, which are
located all around the world,

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:47.050
house the compute, storage,
and networking capabilities

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.490
that power Google's own
products and services,

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:52.690
including Search,
Gmail, and YouTube.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.850
With Google Cloud,
Google is basically

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:56.350
sharing these
computing resources

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:58.870
with developers like yourself
so you can build and run

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:02.330
applications using Google's
computing infrastructure.

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:06.340
One of the key advantages
of cloud computing is scale.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:09.250
Take an example of a retailer
looking to set up a database

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.170
to help manage their
inventory, pricing,

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:14.890
and demand across thousands
of stores internationally.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:16.690
One of the biggest
challenges in retail

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:19.120
is figuring out how to handle
massive seasonal spikes

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.130
during the holiday season.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:22.810
If you were to try
to set up and manage

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:25.240
your own on-premise
database, it would likely

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.180
be expensive and inefficient to
provision additional hardware

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.650
to do so.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.420
You'd have to pay
large upfront costs

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:33.790
to set up the additional
database infrastructure, which

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:36.018
you wouldn't even need
for most of the year.

00:01:36.018 --> 00:01:37.810
You'd also need to
worry about implementing

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:40.630
your own complicated
database charting strategy,

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:43.450
managing backups, meeting
performance requirements,

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:45.760
and securing the entire thing.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.010
Or you could use a
fully managed database

00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:51.550
like Cloud Spanner, where
all this is managed for you.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.530
Google Cloud would
manage scaling for you

00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:57.740
so that you only pay for the
data storage you actually need.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.240
Google Cloud has hundreds of
different products and services

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.980
that solve problems
shared by developers

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.900
all around the world.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.675
While it would take me hours
to talk about every single one,

00:02:06.675 --> 00:02:08.300
here's a look at
several different ways

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.450
that these products can
help you as a developer.

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:12.950
One of the first
things you may want

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:14.720
to do when trying
out a cloud platform

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.480
is hosting your code
and applications.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.460
Compute Engine gives you
virtual machines that

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:21.560
run in Google Data centers.

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:24.740
With Cloud Run, you can deploy
containerized applications

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:27.710
on a fully managed
serverless platform.

00:02:27.710 --> 00:02:30.830
And with App Engine, you can
deploy highly scalable web apps

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.700
and other back-end services.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.670
Next, you probably want
to store data, some of it

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.010
structured, some
of it unstructured.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.260
Cloud Storage is perfect for
unstructured data like images,

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.740
videos, and audio files.

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:48.940
Cloud SQL hosts Google-managed
versions of MySQL, Postgres,

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:50.847
and SQL Server.

00:02:50.847 --> 00:02:52.555
You can run the same
relational databases

00:02:52.555 --> 00:02:56.520
you know without all the
hassles of self-management.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.730
Cloud Firestore is a NoSQL
document-based realtime

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:00.990
database.

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.402
It's popular for use
cases like gaming,

00:03:03.402 --> 00:03:04.860
where it's very
important for users

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:08.540
to have the most up-to-date
version of data in real time.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.105
Then there are AI and
machine learning tools,

00:03:11.105 --> 00:03:13.730
which can be helpful if you are
an experienced machine learning

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.580
developer, but also if you
know zero machine learning,

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.370
but still want to take advantage
of Google's AI technologies

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:21.950
for your own applications.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.630
Suppose you have images--

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:26.030
lots of images in your
application-- and you

00:03:26.030 --> 00:03:27.950
want to enhance your
application by knowing

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:30.740
what's in each image.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:33.530
Vision AI provides an API
that will take an image

00:03:33.530 --> 00:03:37.160
and run object detection,
find landmarks, extract text,

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:39.470
and more, allowing
your users to use

00:03:39.470 --> 00:03:41.190
that information in your app.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.130
Or maybe you want to
analyze user comments

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:46.190
in your application using
natural language processing.

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:49.370
With Cloud Natural Language,
you can analyze a piece of text

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:53.270
and get information about its
entities, sentiment, syntax,

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:56.412
and content categorization.

00:03:56.412 --> 00:03:57.870
And if you want to
build and deploy

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:59.560
your own machine
learning models,

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:01.350
you can do that as well.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.110
Vertex AI is Google
Cloud's unified machine

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:07.620
learning platform for training,
hosting, and managing machine

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:09.660
learning models.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.600
One great aspect of Google
Cloud is the opportunity

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.820
to seamlessly integrate
multiple products to suit

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.850
your own unique use case.

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:19.890
Say that you're building
a new social networking

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.690
site for dogs.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.820
You could start out by
storing profile photos

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.650
in Cloud Storage.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:28.380
Then you could set up a
Cloud Firestore database

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.540
to store other profile
information, like dog

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:33.810
names, locations, and hobbies.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.660
Next, you could use Vision
AI to detect objects

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:40.560
in each photo, like balls,
stuffed animals, and bones.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.690
These machine
learning-powered data insights

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.910
might help your users
find other furry friends

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.320
with similar interests.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:49.650
And finally, you could
deploy your application

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:52.200
to the web using Cloud Run.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.900
This would make it easy for
your app to scale automatically

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:57.880
as you gain more users.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.820
So there you have it,
an overview of what

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.920
Google Cloud is and some
of the different products

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.620
you may be interested
in as a developer.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.950
Stay tuned for more episodes
of Google Cloud Essentials,

00:05:07.950 --> 00:05:10.650
where we'll cover the Google
Cloud Console, the Google Cloud

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:14.980
SDK, the free trial,
key use cases, and more.

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.820
I can't wait to see what
you build with Google Cloud.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:21.170
[MUSIC PLAYING]

